    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1049">

                <pb facs="1146" n="1134" sameAs="frus:frus1944v03/pg1134"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">865.01/2677a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the Soviet
                        Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-06-17T12:00:00-04:00">June 17,
                            1944—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1502. You are requested to inform Molotov personally of the position of this
                    Government in regard to the Bonomi Government as set forth in Department’s
                    telegrams to Kirk, repeated to you as nos. 1485<anchor n="70a" xml:id="d1049-f1"
                    /> and 1484 of June 13 and no. 1498 of June 16.<anchor n="71" xml:id="d1049-f2"
                    />
                </p>
                <pb facs="1147" n="1135" xml:id="pg1135"/>
                <p>In conveying the foregoing to Molotov you should explain to him that this
                    Government has very much in mind the principles set forth in the Declaration on
                    Italy adopted at the Moscow Conference to which the three Governments have
                    subscribed.</p>
                <p>Under the circumstances Allied refusal to permit the inauguration of the Bonomi
                    Government which as you are aware is the first Italian Government composed
                    entirely of representatives of anti-Fascist parties would run directly contrary
                    to the principles contained in the Declaration on Italy and would gravely impair
                    public confidence in the sincerity in the expressed aims and principles of the
                    United Nations. You may express to Molotov the confidence of this Government
                    that the Soviet Government will share our views on this subject.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1049-f1" n="70a" xml:id="d1049-f1-content">
                <p>See footnote 66, <ref target="#pg1131">p. 1131</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1049-f2" n="71" xml:id="d1049-f2-content">
                <p>Department’s telegrams 192 and 206 to Naples, repeated to Moscow as 1484 and
                    1498, respectively, not printed; but for United States position regarding Bonomi
                    government, see <hi rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> to the British
                    Embassy, June 16, <hi rendition="#italic">supra.</hi>
                </p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
